course: dictionary
lesson: 11145
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A wildcat is a wild cat. It looks a bit like a big tabby, and it lives wild in Europe, Africa and Asia. «Cats came to live near people about 10,000 years ago in the Middle East, when farmers stored grain; the grain drew mice, and the mice drew wildcats.» Here the wildcats are animals living in nature, not pets.
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. In the news, «a wildcat strike» means a sudden strike by workers that the union did not officially approve. Wildcat is then a describing word, and it tells you what kind of strike it was.
explain: Always check the words around it. If the sentence is about animals or nature, use the animal meaning. If it is about workers or a strike, use the strike meaning. The context tells you which meaning fits.
fact: A wildcat is a wild cat that looks like a big tabby, living in Europe, Africa and Asia.
fact: The Scottish wildcat is very rare.
fact: In America, wildcat can mean any wild member of the cat family, like a bobcat.
fact: A wildcat strike is a sudden strike by workers without the union's official approval.
fact: House cats come from the African wildcat; people began living with them about 10,000 years ago.
exercise: «House cats come from the African wildcat.» In this sentence, what is a wildcat? | A fierce, wild person | A cat that sleeps in a house | *A wild cat, the ancestor of house cats | A sudden strike by workers
exercise: «The workers held a wildcat strike without the union's approval.» What does wildcat mean here? | A fierce, wild person | *A sudden strike without official approval | A wild cat that lives in Scotland
exercise: Lena reads that the Scottish wildcat is very rare. What does this tell us? | *It is an animal that is hard to find and needs protecting | It is a strike by workers that happens every week | It is a cat that lives in every house in Scotland
test: In a nature book, a wildcat is described as a rare, protected animal. Which meaning fits? | A house cat with a collar | A fierce, wild person | A sudden strike by workers | *A wild cat that looks like a big tabby
test: The news says the wildcat strike started with no warning and no vote. Which meaning fits? | A wild cat living in Africa | A tabby kitten kept as a pet | *A sudden strike without official approval | A fierce, wild person
exam: Which sentence uses wildcat to mean a wild animal? | «The wildcat strike began before the vote.» | «The workers walked out in a wildcat.» | *«A wildcat crept quietly through the long grass.»
